"P442.dvi (https://real.mtak.hu/20177/1/ajc_v59_1_p211_217_u_181146.177653.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.7 years ago; Hence it is not even possible to find \\(n - 1\\) disjoint transversals. ... An interesting counterpoint to Theorem 5 is the unresolved conjecture by Stein ([9], see also [10]) that any \\((n-1)\\times n\\) row-latin rectangle has a transversal.\nHence it is not even possible to find \\(n - 1\\) disjoint transversals. For \\(n \\neq 6\\) we found \\(n - 1\\) disjoint transversals in all the candidates that we tested.\n\nMoreover, we checked exhaustively and found that every \\(7 \\times 8\\) latin rectangle can be decomposed into transversals. We also tried to generalise the structure of some small examples to \\(n > 8\\), but without success. Thus it remains an interesting open question as to whether there are only finitely many examples of the type sought by Häggkvist and Johansson.\n\nIf we relax the conditions slightly it becomes easy to build infinite families of examples, as our next two results show.\n\n**Theorem 5**  *For any \\(n \\ge 3\\) there exists an \\((n-1)\\times n\\) row-latin rectangle that cannot be decomposed into transversals.*\n\n**Proof:** Let \\(R\\) be the row-latin rectangle consisting of \\(n-2\\) rows that are copies of \\([1,2,\\ldots,n]\\), followed by a single row \\([2,3,\\ldots,n-2,n-1,1,n]\\). Now consider any \\(x \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,n-1\\}\\). A transversal that includes symbol \\(x\\) from the last row cannot use any entry in column \\(x\\), so it has to use an entry in the last column. However there are only \\(n-2\\) entries in the last column outside the last row, and these have to be paired with the \\(n-1\\) different values of \\(x\\) in any decomposition of \\(R\\) into transversals. Hence \\(R\\) cannot be decomposed into transversals. □\n\nAn interesting counterpoint to Theorem 5 is the unresolved conjecture by Stein ([9], see also [10]) that any \\((n-1)\\times n\\) row-latin rectangle has a transversal.\n\n**Theorem 6**  *For any odd \\(n \\ge 3\\) there exists an \\((n-1)\\times n\\) generalized latin rectangle that cannot be decomposed into transversals.*\n\n**Proof:** Since \\(n-1\\) is even, there exists a latin square \\(L\\) of order \\(n-1\\) that contains no transversal [10]. Adjoin to \\(L\\) a new column consisting of symbols that are distinct from each other and from the symbols in \\(L\\). This produces an \\((n-1)\\times n\\) generalized latin rectangle. It cannot be decomposed into transversals, since any such decomposition would have to include one transversal of \\(L\\). □--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.21396)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Date: Thu Jul 23 14:58:30 2026\n\nThe target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex.\nTitle: A six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture\nAuthors: Jesse Geneson\nDate: Thu Jul 23 14:58:30 2026\n\nThe target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex. We give an explicit six-neuron counterexample. For every sufficiently small $\\varepsilon>0$, the CTLN defined by one fixed graph at $δ=29\\varepsilon/25$ is nondegenerate and has a stable fixed point with nonclique full support. Its values of $q=δ(1-\\varepsilon)/\\varepsilon$ tend to $29/25$. In the complementary direction, for any CTLN on $n\\geq3$ vertices, we prove that in the parameter range \\[ q\\geq n-2-\\frac{n-3}{2}\\varepsilon, \\] no nonclique support can satisfy both the fixed-point positivity and linear stability conditions. Consequently, throughout this range, every nondegenerate CTLN has exactly its target-free cliques as supports of stable fixed points. In particular, this holds when $\\varepsilon\\leqδ/(δ+n-2)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe target-free clique conjecture for combinatorial threshold-linear networks | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/371376/the-target-free-clique-conjecture-for-combinatorial-threshol)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; # The target-free clique conjecture for combinatorial threshold-linear networks ... The target-free clique conjecture.\n\n# The target-free clique conjecture for combinatorial threshold-linear networks\n\n1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nLet $G$G be a directed graph on $n$n nodes, and let $W=W(G;\\varepsilon,\\delta)$W=W(G;ε,δ) be the associated combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN). A subset $\\sigma\\subseteq[n]$σ⊆[n] is a clique if $G|_{\\sigma}$G∣σ​ has all bidirectional edges between distinct vertices. A vertex $v\\notin\\sigma$v∈/σ is a target of $\\sigma$σ if every $j\\in\\sigma$j∈σ has $j\\to v$j→v in $G$G, and $\\sigma$σ is target-free if it has no external target. Assume\n\n$$\\delta>0,\\qquad 0<\\varepsilon<\\frac{\\delta}{\\delta+1}.$$δ>0,0<ε<δ+1 δ​.\n\nThe target-free clique conjecture. A subset $\\sigma$σ is the support of a stable fixed point if and only if $\\sigma$σ is a target-free clique. Target-free cliques are known to support stable fixed points, and the converse has been proved in several special cases, including oriented and symmetric graphs, networks with at most four nodes, and certain parameter ranges; the general statement remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Jesse Geneson, “Stable non-minimal fixed points of threshold-linear networks”, arXiv:2511.05517 (2025).\n\ncombinatoricsdynamical-systemsthreshold-linear-networks\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2607.21396v1)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: last week; Published: 2026-07-23 ... The target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex.\n\n## A six-neuron counterexample to the target-free clique conjecture\n\nJesse Geneson\n\nPublished: 2026-07-23\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ncs.DM math.CO\n\ngraph\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe target-free clique conjecture asserts that the supports of stable fixed points of a nondegenerate combinatorial threshold-linear network (CTLN) are exactly its target-free cliques: bidirected cliques for which no outside vertex receives an edge from every clique vertex. We give an explicit six-neuron counterexample. For every sufficiently small $\\varepsilon>0$, the CTLN defined by one fixed graph at $δ=29\\varepsilon/25$ is nondegenerate and has a stable fixed point with nonclique full support. Its values of $q=δ(1-\\varepsilon)/\\varepsilon$ tend to $29/25$. In the complementary direction, for any CTLN on $n\\geq3$ vertices, we prove that in the parameter range \\[ q\\geq n-2-\\frac{n-3}{2}\\varepsilon, \\] no nonclique support can satisfy both the fixed-point positivity and linear stability conditions. Consequently, throughout this range, every nondegenerate CTLN has exactly its target-free cliques as supports of stable fixed points. In particular, this holds when $\\varepsilon\\leqδ/(δ+n-2)$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nlatintransv.dvi (https://cs.nyu.edu/~spencer/papers/latintransv.pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Given an \\(n \\times n\\) matrix \\(A = [a_{ij}]\\), a transversal of \\(A\\) is a set of elements, one from each row and one from each column. ... There have been more conjectures than theorems on Latin transversals in the literature. ... Any \\(n \\times n\\) matrix in which no element appears more than \\(s\\) times contains \\(n\\) disjoint latin transversals provided \\(s \\leq \\epsilon n\\) (for \\(\\epsilon\\), an absolute constant \\(\\ll 1\\)).\n# Covering with Latin Transversals\n\nNoga Alon¹, Joel Spencer² and Prasad Tetali³\n\n(first draft)\n\n## 1 Introduction\n\nGiven an \\(n \\times n\\) matrix \\(A = [a_{ij}]\\), a transversal of \\(A\\) is a set of elements, one from each row and one from each column. A transversal is a *Latin transversal* if no two elements are the same. There have been more conjectures than theorems on Latin transversals in the literature. Erdős et al. [6] provide a good overview of the work done on transversals. Previous work can be found also in [10] and [4].\n\nRecently, Erdős and Spencer showed [5] that there always exists a latin transversal in any \\(n \\times n\\) matrix in which no element appears more than \\(s\\) times, for \\(s \\leq (n - 1)/16\\). Here we show that, in fact, all the elements of the matrix can be partitioned into Latin transversals, provided \\(n\\) is a power of 2 and no element appears more than \\(\\epsilon n\\) times for some fixed \\(\\epsilon > 0\\).\n\n**Theorem 1** *Let \\(n\\) be \\(2^m\\). Any \\(n \\times n\\) matrix in which no element appears more than \\(s\\) times contains \\(n\\) disjoint latin transversals provided \\(s \\leq \\epsilon n\\) (for \\(\\epsilon\\), an absolute constant \\(\\ll 1\\)).*\n\nThe assumption that \\(n\\) is a power of 2 can be weakened, but at the moment we are unable to prove the theorem for all values of \\(n\\). On the other hand, our proof can be easily modified to prove the existence of many pairwise disjoint transversals in any \\(n\\) by \\(n\\) matrix in which no entry appears more than \\(\\epsilon n\\) times, without any restriction on \\(n\\). Therefore our method implies a strengthening of the result of [5] for any \\(n\\), (apart from the actual value of the constant \\(\\epsilon\\)).\n\nWe prove the above theorem in a more general framework, stated in terms of graphs.\n\n¹ Bellcore, Morristown, NJ 07960 and Department of Mathematics, Sackler Faculty of Exact Sciences, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv, Israel. Research supported in part by a USA-Israel BSF grant  \n² Department of Computer Science, Courant Institute of Mathematical Sciences, New York, NY 10012. Research supported in part by a USA-Israel BSF grant  \n³ Visitor, DIMACS center, Rutgers University, P.O. Box 1179–Busch Campus, Piscataway, NJ 08855\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnswers to Questions by Denes on Latin Power Sets (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3A78a3c1d3-2927-4ee6-ad16-a26b66380af3/files/me30d97ff8dd0e2cb03f477166400a9a3)\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 25.2 years ago; D´enes has used Latin power sets in an attempt to settle a conjecture by Hall and Paige on complete ... A transversal of a row-Latin square of order n is a selection of n entries of the square ... (iii) L can be decomposed into disjoint transversals,\nSuppose\nthat p and q are two permutations in the symmetric group Sn. Let Li j = k if and only if\ngp(i) ⊗gq( j) = gk. The matrix L = (Li j) is a Latin square, and we say it is based on G. In\nthe special case when g1 = ε and both p and q are the identity permutation we say that L is a\nCayley table for G. Note that the ﬁrst row and column of a Cayley table are in natural order,\nbut the remainder of the square depends on the enumeration chosen for the elements of G.\nAlso, note that the squares based on a given group form an isotopy class.\nA complete mapping of G is a permutation σ of the elements of G such that the map τ\ndeﬁned by τ(x) = x⊗σ(x) is also a permutation of the elements of G. See [3] for background\non all the concepts just introduced and for a proof of this standard result.\nTHEOREM 1. Let L be a Latin square based on a ﬁnite group G. The following statements\nare equivalent.\n(i) G has a complete mapping,\n(ii) L has a transversal,\n(iii) L can be decomposed into disjoint transversals,\n(iv) there exists a Latin square orthogonal to L.\nHall and Paige [9] attempted to identify conditions under which a ﬁnite group would have\na complete mapping. They made the following conjecture.\n0195–6698/01/071009 + 12\n$35.00/0\nc⃝2001 Academic Press--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConsidered but not curated · AI Proof Watch (https://aiproofwatch.com/sources/considered?page=30)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; ### The Target-Free Clique Conjecture for Threshold-Linear Networks ... Screening recorded: 2026-10-17 · Decision reviewed: 2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z ... The original tight-Hamilton-cycle paper states a quasirandom 3-graph theorem but the readable manuscript contains no AI disclosure for its proof; the batch metadata supplies no central-origin evidence.\n### The Target-Free Clique Conjecture for Threshold-Linear Networks\n\nVibeMathed\n\nOn hold after review\n\nThe acknowledgments say Codex and Claude Code helped with proof exploration and criticism, but do not identify a model-origin six-neuron counterexample or central proof step.\n\nScreening recorded: 2026-10-17 · Decision reviewed: 2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z\n\n### Four-Terminal Planar Case of the Dinitz-Garg-Goemans Cost Conjectu